    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The \"howling\" sound is typically caused by air entering the turbine section at such a high velocity that it creates solenoid-induced pressure waves rather than just simple friction. When exhaust flow exceeds the turbine's optimal surge limit or encounters restrictive timing valves, the moving air creates standing pressure waves that amplify into a high-pitched whistle or screech. This is an aerodynamic resonance effect, a specific type of flow instability, rather than the mechanical friction you might expect from metal-on-metal contact.",
